.TH FAAH 1 "2026-11-02" "faah 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
faah \- play the bundled faah sound
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B faah
.RI [ \-\- ]
.RI [ player-option ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B faah
plays the short audio clip shipped in
.IR /usr/share/faah/faah.mp3 .
It does not implement its own audio decoding: it finds a suitable player
on the system and hands the file over to it.
.PP
Players are tried in this order:
.BR mpg123 ,
.BR mpg321 ,
.BR ffplay ,
.BR mpv ,
.BR cvlc ,
.BR gst-play-1.0 ,
.BR mplayer ,
.BR play ,
and
.BR afplay .
The first one found is used.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
Stop looking for faah's own options.  Everything after it is passed to
the audio player.
.PP
Any argument that is not one of the options above is passed to the
player before the file name.  These arguments are player specific.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FAAH_AUDIO
Play this file instead of the bundled clip.
.TP
.B FAAH_PLAYER
Use this program as the audio player instead of auto-detection.  The
value is treated as a single command name, not a shell command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/faah/faah.mp3
The bundled audio clip.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The clip was played successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The audio file could not be read or no player was found.
.PP
Any other status is the exit status of the audio player.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B faah
Play the clip once.
.TP
.B faah \-\-loop 3
Play the clip three times, using the mpg123 loop option.
.TP
.B FAAH_PLAYER=mpv faah
Force the use of
.BR mpv .
